## Test and Debug Commands

### Rebuild whole stack and drop DB

```powershell
docker compose down -v
docker compose up --build
```


### Start

```powershell
docker compose up --build
```


### Stop

```powershell
docker compose down
```


### Check embedded dimensions

```powershell
docker compose exec bot python -m scripts.check_embed_dim
```

When using Gemini embeds, the expected embedding dimension is `3072`. If you swap engines later, update the classes selected in `services/ai/selector.py` and make sure the vector dimension still matches your replacement embedding model.

If the dimension changes, update `vector(3072)` in `services/schema.sql` to the new size, then rebuild with a fresh DB:

```powershell
docker compose down -v
docker compose up --build
```


### Ingest data into DB

```powershell
docker compose exec bot python -m services.rag.ingest_class_docs {class_id} /app/data/classes/{class_id}
```


### Test ingested data and semantic search

```powershell
docker compose exec -e PYTHONPATH=/app -e TEST_CLASS_ID={class_id} bot python scripts/test_retrieve.py
```


### Count ingested rows in `class_docs`

```powershell
docker exec -it csbot-db psql -U app -d rag -c "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM class_docs WHERE class_id='{class_id}';"
```


### Inspect tags

```powershell
docker exec -it csbot-db psql -U app -d rag -c "SELECT id, source_ref, tags FROM class_docs WHERE class_id='{class_id}' LIMIT 20;"
```

If no metadata file exists, the ingestion logic assumes the file name as the default tag.


### Preview stored embeddings

```powershell
docker exec -it csbot-db psql -U app -d rag -c "SELECT id, LEFT(embedding::text, 200) AS embedding_preview FROM class_docs LIMIT 3;"
```


## Notes

- When you change code, it is safest to stop Docker and rebuild before testing again.
- The `scripts/` folder is for development/testing utilities and is not part of normal runtime behavior.
- If you swap to a different embedding model later, re-check the embedding dimension and update the DB vector column size to match.
- The chatbot now uses DB-backed class retrieval and thread memory, so course files need to be ingested for the best experience.
